        "math_academy_id": "q-347614",
        "problem": "Find the first three non-zero terms in the Taylor expansion of $\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}$ about the point $x=2.$\n\n$\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}=${{field-1}}\n$-\\,$\n{{field-2}}\n$(x-2)\\,+\\,$\n{{field-3}}\n$(x-2)^{2}-\u22ef$",
        "worked_solution": "We use Taylor's theorem in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=f(a)+{f}^{\u2032}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{{f}^{\u2033}(a)}{2!}(x-a)^{2}+\\frac{{f}^{\u2034}(a)}{3!}(x-a)^{3}+\u22ef,\n$$\n\nwith $f(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}$ and $a=2.$ \n\nComputing the required derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}={x}^{-2} \\\\ f(2) & ={2}^{-2}=\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ {f}^{\u2032}(x) & =-\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}=-2{x}^{-3} \\\\ {f}^{\u2032}(2) & =-2(2)^{-3}=-\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ {f}^{\u2033}(x) & =\\frac{6}{{x}^{4}}=6{x}^{-4} \\\\ {f}^{\u2033}(2) & =6(2)^{-4}=\\frac{3}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{1}{4}+(-\\frac{1}{4})(x-2)+\\frac{(\\frac{3}{8})}{2!}(x-2)^{2}+\\frac{(-\\frac{3}{4})}{3!}(x-2)^{3}+\u22ef \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{1}{4}(x-2)+\\frac{3}{16}(x-2)^{2}-\u22ef.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
